Regression tests for SPARTA rigid-body surface objects (fix rigid)
-------------------------------------------------------------------

run_tests.py runs a set of small input decks and checks the results
against analytic expectations with tolerances:

ballistic     free body with initial velocity + angular momentum:
              straight-line COM motion, constant angular velocity
rotation      torque-free tumbling of an asymmetric 3d body: the
              angular velocity must vary (polhode motion) while the
              rotational energy is conserved, and rotate richardson
              must conserve it far better than rotate euler
force         same body under a constant external force: COM follows
              the exact constant-force trajectory to output precision
bounce        body vs static wall via push-off springs: elastic
              rebound within 1%, no tunneling into the cutoff zone
restitution   analytic properties of the push-off contact model:
              elastic contact rebounds with restitution 1, a linear
              spring-dashpot restitution is independent of impact speed,
              a Hertzian one depends on it
momentum      gas + body momentum conservation in a periodic box:
              the lag-corrected invariant holds to 1e-11
recoil        one particle hits a body only 10x heavier, head-on,
              off-center (spins it) and in 3d, and a body of equal
              mass: exact two-body elastic result and energy
              conservation to 1e-10
overrun       body sweeping 40% of a cell per step through resting
              gas: no particles overrun (deletion counter constant)
remap         cutcell and incremental remap modes produce identical
              deterministic trajectories
multiremap    two gas-driven bodies give identical trajectories in
              cutcell and incremental modes (multi-body incremental)
transplane    body sweeping 1.4 cells per step over cells cut only by
              a transparent plane: total flow volume per step identical
              in both remap modes (engulfed and vacated uncut cells)
staticdist    gas-driven body next to a 200-segment static circle:
              identical trajectories in both remap modes, no particles
              deleted; on several procs with --dist most static surfs
              are ghost surfs, exercising ghost-cell collisions
staticdist3d  3d: cube body drifting past a 1200-triangle static sphere,
              identical trajectories in both remap modes, no deletions;
              with --dist on several procs the sphere is mostly ghost surfs
restart       restart continuation: a deterministic push-off run of two
              bodies split across write_restart/read_restart, with each body's state
              carried by the fix outfile/infile, must reproduce the
              one-shot trajectory, including splits made while the body
              is in contact with the wall
gridchange    the grid changing underneath the body: a no-particle
              push-off trajectory must be identical with and without
              fix balance (random style) and fix adapt, which rebuild
              and re-partition the grid mid-run
exitbox       two bodies leave the box through opposite faces: the
              inside/outside marking of a surf-free grid is decided by
              parity, the run completes and the motion is ballistic
splitcell     body sweeping alongside a diagonal wall that creates
              split cells: particles entering the swept split cells
              are reflected (no deletions), both remap modes agree
twobody       two bodies colliding head-on via push-off: symmetric,
              elastic within 2%
splitbalance  split cells plus a fix balance every 3 steps: the particle
              count must not change (a stale sort after the re-map let
              the balance migrate cells with duplicated particles)
pushpair      ASYMMETRIC body-body contact (heavy large body vs light
              small body, corner-vs-face): total pair momentum
              conserved to output precision, verifying that contact
              forces are applied equal-and-opposite to both bodies
badmoi        negative: 3d moi violating the triangle inequality
              must be rejected
notwatertight negative: open body group must be rejected
zerothick     negative: a watertight body enclosing no area (2d) or
              volume (3d), e.g. a doubled line or tri, must be rejected
inward        body surfs traversed the wrong way round (normals pointing
              inward, a container) are rejected in 2d and 3d
refix         a fix rigid re-defined between runs, then balance_grid
              before the next run: the rigid map rebuild must not reach
              the deleted fix (use-after-free visible under ASAN)
facetbounce   a square rebounding from a 200-segment static circle via
              push-off: kinetic energy in free flight after the contact
              equals the launch energy to 1e-4 (contacts with a faceted
              curved surface must be conservative)
vacate        gas collisions (collide vss) with a fast body spanning whole
              interior cells: the sliver of an interior cell the body
              uncovers within a step holds particles at zero flow volume
              until the end-of-step re-cut, and the collision step must
              skip such a cell rather than abort; Np constant, no deletions
prenofix      negative: a fix rigid re-defined between runs, then
              "run pre no" (setup skipped): the uninitialized fix must
              refuse to run rather than advance a deleted body's state
modifyafter   negative: with distributed surfs, surf_modify of the body
              surfs after the fix is defined must be rejected
wallmotion    negative: a body surf_collide model with its own wall
              motion (diffuse translate) must be rejected
customemit    a fix emit/surf spreading a custom per-surf attribute,
              defined before fix rigid, over two runs: the per-surf
              status flags fix rigid resets after a grid rebuild must
              be reset collectively, else the second run hangs (dist)
emitsurf      negative: a fix emit/surf emitting from the body surfs
              must be rejected
renumber      negative: a remove_surf issued after the fix is defined
              which re-numbers the body surfs must be rejected, with
              distributed surfs and (with a read_surf restoring the
              surf count) non-distributed surfs
rotwall       a collision with a body spinning at 0.09 radian per step
              must be independent of the timestep: the body is heavy
              enough not to be perturbed by it and spins at a constant
              rate, so nothing else in the problem depends on the
              timestep.  the 3d variant does the same for a spinning
              cube and the moving-triangle test
rotwall3d     see rotwall
mixture       negative: a compute surf whose mixture omits a species
              must be rejected, since the body would feel only part of
              the gas
badinfile     negative: an infile with a malformed number (1.0d-22)
              must be rejected rather than read as 1.0
react         a surf_react model on the body surfs which leaves exactly
              one particle: species A becomes species B of twice the mass,
              so the impulse is mpost*v - mpre*vpre and the recoil must use
              the post-collision mass and the matrix branch; checked
              against the exact rigid-body impulse solution
badreact      negative: a reaction which destroys the particle (or produces
              a second one) must be rejected on body surfs
recoilspin    the recoil correction must include the body's ROTATIONAL
              response, not just 1/M: an off-centre strike with Izz chosen
              so (r x n)^2/Izz equals 1/M, checked against the exact
              rigid-body impulse solution for both vcom and omega, plus
              exact momentum and elastic energy conservation
couple        accuracy of the explicit gas-body coupling on one impulse:
              the body VELOCITY is exact at every timestep, while the
              POSITION carries an offset of at most one step's body
              motion which must fall linearly with dt
densityfar    dstyle = density must be placement invariant: the same unit
              cube as the density test, moved to (1e5,1e5,1e5), held to
              the same tolerances.  an origin-referenced moment sum keeps
              only about (L/R)^2 of full precision and fails this
axistuck      no rigid body: axisymmetric tube in which particles hit
              the same static line many times per step; the mover must
              not treat the repeated hits as a stuck particle
tallyorder    a fix ave/surf on a static wall defined after fix rigid
              must see the same hit counts as one defined before it
              (KOKKOS: tallies fetched before the grid rebuild)
crossproc     three gas-driven bodies crossing the whole box, with
              contacts between them and with the box boundaries: with
              distributed surfs each proc stores local copies only of
              the bodies which can reach its cells, appended as a body
              approaches, and the trajectories must be identical to the
              non-distributed run on the same procs.  run it on several
              procs, since on one proc every body is always local
splitmany     three faceted circles drifting and spinning across a grid
              whose cells are about the size of their segments, so a body
              vertex repeatedly leaves two disconnected flow slivers in a
              cell: a split cell which appears and disappears within a
              step or two, and whose sub cells the incremental re-cut
              must create and remove itself rather than fall back to a
              full grid re-map.  without particles the two remap modes
              must agree at every step; with a collisional gas the
              particle count must hold and nothing may be deleted
nbody         four squares of different masses colliding with each other
              in pair and multi-body push-off contacts, no particles, no
              wall contacts: total linear momentum and total angular
              momentum about the origin are conserved exactly, kinetic
              energy within 2%
idperm        the same three colliding squares numbered (1,2,3) and
              (3,1,2): each body follows the same trajectory, so nothing
              depends on the order of the body element table or of the
              per-body loops
missingid     negative: surf types 1 and 3 with no type 2 must be
              rejected, since body 2 would have no surface elements

axisymmetric domains: a rigid body is a body of revolution about the
x axis, so it has two degrees of freedom, axial translation and axial
spin, and the transverse force and torque the gas exerts average to
zero around the ring a simulated particle stands for

axiballistic  free body of revolution: linear drift along the axis and
              constant spin, with the transverse components identically
              zero rather than merely small; also the exact
              constant-force trajectory
axidensity    dstyle = density for a body of revolution: mass, COM and
              inertia from the volume of revolution, against a cylinder
              (no dr terms) and a cone (all of them), both of which a
              straight profile represents exactly
aximomentum   axial momentum of gas + body is conserved, the same
              lag-corrected invariant as the momentum test.  only the
              axial component is a conserved quantity of the
              axisymmetric model at all
axispin       axial angular momentum of gas + body is conserved while a
              spinning body spins down against a diffuse wall.  this is
              the invariant that pins the azimuthal channel of the
              recoil, kmat[2][2] = r^2/ixx
axidrag       free-molecular drag on a sphere of revolution against the
              analytic rho U^2 pi R^2, and, more sharply, the same
              sphere run as a rigid body and as a static surf must
              agree to round-off: with the body at rest the moving-surf
              test reduces to the static one exactly, because the body
              frame is a Galilean boost along the symmetry axis.  also
              run with radial cell weighting
axipush       push-off contact for a body of revolution: elastic axial
              bounce off a static disk, while a live radial contact with
              the outer boundary must never reach the body.  the axis is
              not a wall, and the contact force is ring-weighted
axipair       push-off contact between TWO bodies of revolution on the
              same axis.  the ring weighting cancels between action and
              reaction, so the pair's axial momentum is conserved exactly
              however it is chosen; the individual velocities reach the
              elastic two-body result to the one-step contact resolution
axireact      a mass-changing surface reaction on a SPINNING body of
              revolution.  specular reflection leaves v_theta relative to
              the wall unchanged, so the azimuthal impulse is exactly
              (mpost - mpre)*v_theta, non-zero only because the mass
              changed; the body answers through kmat[2][2] = r^2/ixx.
              both exact invariants hold with the per-particle mass taken
              from the current species
axibadvcom    a transverse vcom must be rejected: it would carry the
              body off the axis
axiopen       the axis exception must not let through a free end that is
              off the axis, even one on the box surface, which the Surf
              class's own watertight check does allow

The decks with several bodies (twobody, pushpair, exitbox, multiremap,
axipair, and the restart trio) define them with one fix rigid using
bodystyle type: each read_surf gives its body a distinct surf type via
typeadd, and the per-body parameters come from a data.*.bodies infile.

The ballistic, force, bounce, twobody, and pushpair tests contain no
particles, and the recoil test a single particle; they must produce
identical results on any number of procs.
The momentum and overrun tests run through statistical gas, but their
checks are exact: the lag-corrected momentum invariant holds to 1e-11
and the overrun deletion count must be exactly zero.

Usage:

  python3 run_tests.py --exe ../../../src/spa_serial
  python3 run_tests.py --exe ../../../src/spa_mpi --mpi "mpirun -np 4"
  python3 run_tests.py --exe ../../../build/src/spa_kokkos_omp --args "-k on -sf kk"

The --args string is passed to SPARTA after the executable, e.g. to
run the KOKKOS package (fix rigid/kk).  A KOKKOS build with
SPARTA_KOKKOS_EXACT reproduces the non-KOKKOS results exactly.

Passing --dist reruns the subset of tests whose decks support
distributed surfs (global surfs explicit/distributed): all but the
negative tests, recoil, restart, and axistuck (no rigid body, a
single grid cell).  Run it on several procs
as well as on one, since only then are static surfs stored as ghost
surfs on some procs.

Exit code = number of failed tests.
